     "title": "The women's rights movement, 1848",
     "type": "SAQ 2: primary source",
     "minutes": 13,
     "stimulus": "<blockquote>“We hold these truths to be self-evident: that all men and women are created equal; that they are endowed by their Creator with certain inalienable rights; that among these are life, liberty, and the pursuit of happiness… The history of mankind is a history of repeated injuries and usurpations on the part of man toward woman…”<cite>Declaration of Sentiments, Seneca Falls Convention, 1848</cite></blockquote>",
     "parts": [
      {
       "label": "a",
       "points": 1,
       "prompt": "Briefly describe ONE historical context for the Declaration of Sentiments.",
       "rubric": [
        "Describes relevant context. Examples: the Second Great Awakening encouraging reform activism; women's experience in the abolitionist movement, such as Stanton and Mott's exclusion at the 1840 World Anti-Slavery Convention; the ideology of “republican motherhood” and “separate spheres” limiting women's public roles; laws denying married women property rights (coverture)."
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "b",
       "points": 1,
       "prompt": "Briefly explain ONE way the authors used the Declaration of Independence to make their argument.",
       "rubric": [
        "Explains the rhetorical strategy: by copying the Declaration's structure and language, adding “and women” and listing grievances against “man” instead of King George, the authors claimed women's rights were part of the nation's founding principles. That made their demands harder to dismiss."
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "c",
       "points": 1,
       "prompt": "Briefly explain ONE development in the period 1865 to 1920 that shows the extent to which the goals of the Declaration of Sentiments were achieved.",
       "rubric": [
        "Explains a development with specific evidence. Examples: the split over the 15th Amendment (1870) when women were excluded from voting protections, and the formation of NWSA and AWSA; Wyoming Territory granting women suffrage (1869); the Nineteenth Amendment (1920) securing the vote, achieving one central goal while other inequalities remained."
       ]
      }
     ],
